{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Have I conceived all this people?</b> — According to Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 11:12), like \"upon the blessings of my father\" (Genesis 49:26), for the father is called הוֹרֶה because he gives and brings about the pregnancy. He says: am I their father or their mother, that I gave birth to them? And the masters of the plain sense said the reverse: have I — am I their mother, that I was pregnant with them and gave birth? And thus \"she conceived Miriam and Shammai\" (1 Chronicles 4:17) — she became pregnant and gave birth to them. Or: am I their father, that I begot him, like \"and Noah begot\" (Genesis 6:10), \"your father who begot you\" (Proverbs 23:22), and similar others. And he said <b>as the nursing father carries the suckling child</b> — the father or the mother. And in my opinion it is all a metaphor about the mother, and its meaning is like: have I conceived all this people, and did I give birth to him? And he mentioned this because a woman endures great pain for children in her remembrance — what she endured on their account from birth and from the womb and from pregnancy. And he said <b>אוֹמֵן</b> (nursing father) because it is אוֹמֵן (masculine form), not אוֹמֶנֶת (feminine form)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "In the phrase 'in her remembrance,' the Hebrew בְּזָכְרָהּ is unusual; I have rendered it literally. The sense appears to be that a woman, in remembering her children, relives the suffering she endured for them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:25.044911464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}